# Byte-pinned files: a gate compares their exact SHA-256 against a hash recorded when they were captured or
# generated, so git must reproduce those bytes on every checkout instead of rewriting line endings for the
# platform. Without this, an LF blob materialises as CRLF wherever core.autocrlf=true (the default on Windows CI
# runners): the hash changes and the gate fails, while still passing in a working tree that happens to hold LF.
# That is exactly how the reference-render gate reached CI looking green and failed there.
#
# The archive is not uniform — it records what Visual Studio actually wrote, and four of the captured sources
# were written with CRLF. Every rule below was measured against the manifests (381 pinned files across 137
# captures, plus 5 fixtures) rather than assumed, so changing one means re-measuring, not guessing.

# Captured Visual Studio evidence. Default to the blob's own bytes...
docs/v2/reference-traces/** -text
# ...then restore CRLF for the four captures that Visual Studio wrote that way.
docs/v2/reference-traces/**/GroupMoveForm.cs text eol=crlf
docs/v2/reference-traces/**/GroupMoveForm.Designer.cs text eol=crlf
docs/v2/reference-traces/**/S100AdapterRoundTripForm.cs text eol=crlf
docs/v2/reference-traces/**/S100AdapterRoundTripForm.Designer.cs text eol=crlf

# The fixtures Visual Studio was pointed at when those traces were captured.
fixtures/VisualStudioReference/** -text

# The Extension Host S100 scenario copies this sample verbatim into its disposable workspace and then compares the
# copy's SHA-256 against the archived capture, so the sample is pinned to the archive's bytes too.
docs/v2/adapter-manifest.sample.json -text

# Generated protocol bindings: `generate-v2-protocol.mjs --check` compares each file against the generator's
# output character for character, and the generator writes "\n".
extension/src/v2Protocol.ts -text
engine/V2Protocol.cs -text
engine-net48/V2Protocol.cs -text
